A||||14||baldlobo||The automatic Trans.||||5||
Z||000000||baldlobo||08-05-2000||04:00 PM||||where there different automatic Transmissions put into the fiero in 1984?||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||08-05-2000||05:52 PM||||Nope...they were all TH125C.<P>If there's something else in there, GM didn't put it in.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=64144&a=464935&p=25646990"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||baldlobo||08-05-2000||07:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>Nope...they were all TH125C.<P>If there's something else in there, GM didn't put it in.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>that's what I have. I was just wondering, if you could by pass the computer to ingage the converter lock up?||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||08-05-2000||07:36 PM||||Yes, ECM converter lockup can be over ridden to both lock or disable locking. This is however no good for the tranny in most cases. The ECM does a very good job of controlling this function.<P>If not done correctly is both extremely bad for the tranny as well as extremely dangerous. Locking at the wrong time will also hurt overall performance of the car. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||baldlobo||08-05-2000||10:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Yes, ECM converter lockup can be over ridden to both lock or disable locking. This is however no good for the tranny in most cases. The ECM does a very good job of controlling this function.<P>If not done correctly is both extremely bad for the tranny as well as extremely dangerous. Locking at the wrong time will also hurt overall performance of the car. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanx, This is how i was going to do it, I was going to splice into the fusebox run wire from the fusebox to an on/off switch mounted some where close to the driver seat and run wire to the lockup actuator: would this work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||08-06-2000||04:46 PM||||No. It won't.<P>You're best bet, performance wise, is to look up Transmision Exchange Co's webs site and get a TC delay unit from them. They aren't very expensive and simply plug in between the tranny and the wiring harness.<P>The delay unit will hold off TC clutch activation a few seconds after the ECM tells it to lock. That will help with some minor drivability problems. It can help performance.<P>Things to keep in mind. <P>The TCC is only supposed to be locked in third gear. (Altho acording to some of my notes not all TH125's have the third gear switch.) Locking the clutch will beat the gears pretty hard as the tranny shifts. It won't take long to break a 125 like this. (TH125 has a number of built in weaknesses. the older the 125 the more problems built in.)<P>The TC actually amplifies engine torque. You get more power out of the TC than you put in, If the TC is working right. The TC locks only at low loads where the multiplier effect is negligable.||||reg||1
Z||000006||baldlobo||08-06-2000||04:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>No. It won't.<P>You're best bet, performance wise, is to look up Transmision Exchange Co's webs site and get a TC delay unit from them. They aren't very expensive and simply plug in between the tranny and the wiring harness.<P>The delay unit will hold off TC clutch activation a few seconds after the ECM tells it to lock. That will help with some minor drivability problems. It can help performance.<P>Things to keep in mind. <P>The TCC is only supposed to be locked in third gear. (Altho acording to some of my notes not all TH125's have the third gear switch.) Locking the clutch will beat the gears pretty hard as the tranny shifts. It won't take long to break a 125 like this. (TH125 has a number of built in weaknesses. the older the 125 the more problems built in.)<P>The TC actually amplifies engine torque. You get more power out of the TC than you put in, If the TC is working right. The TC locks only at low loads where the multiplier effect is negligable.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks theogre for everything. I have now learned at least one new thing today.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||08-06-2000||10:01 PM||||The TCC switch also allows more fluid to flow to the radiator for cooling purposes. If it doesen't lock up at all it spells low transmission life.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||08-06-2000||10:15 PM||||Indiana... Where did you get your info?<BR>I understood that the only ill effects were due to heat generated by TC slippage. (And a slight increase in fuel consumption.) How does the TCC being unlocked cause a decrease in fluid flow to the cooler? Have an ex G.F. that's been driving a 3.1 Lumina with a disconnected TCC for 5-6 years with no ill effects. Both of my 88s have them disconnected until I get around to replacing the solenoids. Can you elaborate?<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."||||reg||1
Z||000009||V8Fiero2000GP||08-07-2000||07:22 PM||||To answer the question if there were different automatics-Yes.  They were all T125...but they differed in gear ratio from year to year.  For example the 1984 auto fiero would run at different RPM at 60 mph than an 87 auto at that speed.  The 1984, for example had a 3.18 gear ratio and the 1987 a 3.33.  Same name, same look transmissions but, with different gear ratios yeilding different RPM's||||reg||1
